<!-- THREE PILLARS -->
<section class="pillars" id="pillars">
  <div class="section-eyebrow">Core Architecture</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Three Technological Pillars</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">The foundational technologies that make orange.Os the operating system of choice for institutions where failure is not an option.</p>

  <div class="pillars-grid reveal">
    <div class="pillar-card">
      <div class="pillar-num">I</div>
      <div class="pillar-icon">⬡</div>
      <div class="pillar-name">ZFS File System</div>
      <div class="pillar-tag">Data Integrity Layer</div>
      <p class="pillar-body">
        The world's most robust data management subsystem. ZFS operates with end-to-end checksums on every block, detecting and repairing silent data corruption before it reaches your application. RAID-Z, snapshotting, and native compression are built-in, not bolted on. Your data heals itself.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="pillar-card">
      <div class="pillar-num">II</div>
      <div class="pillar-icon">◈</div>
      <div class="pillar-name">DTrace</div>
      <div class="pillar-tag">Dynamic Instrumentation</div>
      <p class="pillar-body">
        Observe and diagnose any kernel or userspace behavior in real-time on a live production server with negligible overhead. No reboots, no debugging builds. DTrace gives engineers atomic-level visibility into system calls, memory, I/O, and network — while the server continues serving traffic.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="pillar-card">
      <div class="pillar-num">III</div>
      <div class="pillar-icon">◫</div>
      <div class="pillar-name">Solaris Zones</div>
      <div class="pillar-tag">OS-level Virtualization</div>
      <p class="pillar-body">
        Partition one physical server into hundreds of fully isolated, secure environments — each with its own network stack, filesystem, and process namespace. The conceptual ancestor of Docker and Linux containers, Zones predate container technology by a decade and remain the gold standard for density and isolation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FEATURES: SMF & DNS -->
<section id="network">
  <div class="section-eyebrow">Service Management</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Network Security &amp; SMF</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">In orange.Os, DNS is not a text file. It is a protected system service — monitored, guarded, and immutable to network-level attackers.</p>

  <div class="features-grid">
    <div class="feature-item reveal">
      <div class="feature-icon-wrap">⟳</div>
      <div>
        <div class="feature-title">Smart Service Management (SMF)</div>
        <p class="feature-body">Every system service — DNS, NTP, routing — runs under the SMF supervisor. If a service crashes, SMF detects the failure and restarts it automatically within milliseconds. Dependency ordering ensures services come back up in the correct sequence without human intervention.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-item reveal">
      <div class="feature-icon-wrap">🔒</div>
      <div>
        <div class="feature-title">Anti-Hijack DNS Protection</div>
        <p class="feature-body">DNS configuration lives in the SMF service repository — a structured, access-controlled database. Local network software, DHCP responses, or attackers cannot overwrite your resolver settings by modifying a flat text file. Your external DNS definitions are enforced at the OS level.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-item reveal">
      <div class="feature-icon-wrap">⊕</div>
      <div>
        <div class="feature-title">Independent DNS Architecture</div>
        <p class="feature-body">Where Linux uses <code style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--amber);font-size:0.8em">/etc/resolv.conf</code> — a simple text file any process can overwrite — orange.Os treats DNS as a first-class managed service. Configuring an independent external resolver like a private 192.x server persists across reboots and network changes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-item reveal">
      <div class="feature-icon-wrap">⛊</div>
      <div>
        <div class="feature-title">Role-Based Access Administration</div>
        <p class="feature-body">The RBAC system provides fine-grained privilege delegation. Administrators receive exactly the permissions their role requires — no more. Root access is audited, accountable, and traceable down to individual commands and timestamps stored in tamper-evident logs.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

