<!-- CROSSBOW / ZONES -->
<section class="network-section" id="zones">
  <div class="network-bg"></div>
  <div class="section-eyebrow">Crossbow Architecture</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Virtual Networking &amp; Zones</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">One cable. One server. Hundreds of isolated, bandwidth-controlled environments — each with its own encrypted network stack.</p>

  <div class="network-layout">
    <div class="network-cards">
      <div class="net-card reveal">
        <div class="net-indicator"></div>
        <div>
          <div class="net-title">Absolute Bandwidth Control</div>
          <p class="net-body">Set hard bandwidth ceilings per container, per application, or per virtual NIC. A runaway process or DDoS attempt against one zone is capped at its allocation — the rest of your infrastructure continues at full speed. Hardware enforcement, not software throttling.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="net-card reveal">
        <div class="net-indicator"></div>
        <div>
          <div class="net-title">Virtual Network Fabric — Zero Hardware</div>
          <p class="net-body">Create hundreds of virtual switches, routers, and NICs without purchasing a single additional piece of hardware. Full layer-2 and layer-3 topologies within the same physical server. Crossbow virtualizes the entire network stack inside the OS kernel.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="net-card reveal">
        <div class="net-indicator"></div>
        <div>
          <div class="net-title">Hardware-Accelerated IPsec &amp; TLS</div>
          <p class="net-body">Encryption is offloaded to the processor's cryptographic units. IPsec and TLS encrypt all transit data at wire speed — your network throughput is not diminished by the computational cost of protection. Security does not come at the price of performance.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ZONES DIAGRAM -->
